19
00:01:23.137 --> 00:01:29.746
A precise definition makes later examples
easier to evaluate and revise.

20
00:01:29.746 --> 00:01:36.705
Name each dependency explicitly, and keep
labels and representations consistent

21
00:01:36.705 --> 00:01:38.793
throughout the analysis.

22
00:01:38.906 --> 00:01:46.286
With the foundation in place, examine how
Course Schedule and Format operates in a

23
00:01:46.286 --> 00:01:47.340
concrete sequence.

24
00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:54.287
Completion of this seminar and the related
assignments are required before a CS student

25
00:01:54.287 --> 00:02:00.737
can be registered for co-op. Consider this
worked case: Students attend one 50-minute

26
00:02:00.737 --> 00:02:05.699
lecture session each semester to cover
orientation and policy reviews.

27
00:02:05.699 --> 00:02:13.027
Follow every arrow in order and name the state
before and after each transition.

28
00:02:13.027 --> 00:02:18.762
The trace converts an abstract statement into
an inspectable sequence.

29
00:02:18.866 --> 00:02:26.185
Next, compare the choices involved in Co-op
Preparation and Job Search and make the

30
00:02:26.185 --> 00:02:27.753
decision criteria explicit.

31
00:02:27.753 --> 00:02:35.442
Students are introduced to RIT's Office of
Career Services and Cooperative Education, and

32
00:02:35.442 --> 00:02:41.948
learn about professional, legal, and ethical
responsibilities for their co-op and

33
00:02:41.948 --> 00:02:43.722
subsequent professional experiences.

34
00:02:43.722 --> 00:02:51.735
Consider this worked case: Students draft a
targeted resume and practice technical

35
00:02:51.735 --> 00:02:56.409
interview responses using industry-standard
co-op application formats.

36
00:02:56.409 --> 00:03:03.440
Compare alternatives against the same
constraints so the evaluation does not move

37
00:03:03.440 --> 00:03:04.612
the goalposts.

38
00:03:04.612 --> 00:03:11.253
Using shared criteria turns a preference into
a defensible technical choice.

39
00:03:11.378 --> 00:03:17.455
Apply Career Services and Cooperative
Education Overview to a worked situation

40
00:03:17.455 --> 00:03:21.874
instead of treating it as an isolated
definition.

41
00:03:21.874 --> 00:03:24.124
They should respect privacy.

42
00:03:24.124 --> 00:03:31.843
Consider this worked case: Students learn how
to access career counseling, co-op placement

43
00:03:31.843 --> 00:03:34.812
portals, and alumni networking resources.

44
00:03:34.812 --> 00:03:42.546
Work from the concrete inputs to the result,
checking units, types, or invariants at each

45
00:03:42.546 --> 00:03:43.062
step.

46
00:03:43.062 --> 00:03:48.062
The worked case shows both how to apply the
method and how to check it.

47
00:03:48.170 --> 00:03:55.285
Examine the tradeoffs in Professional, Legal,
and Ethical Responsibilities, including what

48
00:03:55.285 --> 00:03:59.166
improves and what becomes more difficult.

49
00:03:59.166 --> 00:04:05.926
They should maintain high standards of
professional competence, conduct, and ethical

50
00:04:05.926 --> 00:04:06.541
practice.

51
00:04:06.541 --> 00:04:13.760
Consider this worked case: Students review
confidentiality agreements and data handling

52
00:04:13.760 --> 00:04:17.041
protocols required by co-op employers.

53
00:04:17.041 --> 00:04:23.729
State the benefit, the cost, and the condition
under which the tradeoff is acceptable.

54
00:04:23.729 --> 00:04:31.104
Making the tradeoff visible prevents a local
improvement from hiding a larger cost.

55
00:04:31.104 --> 00:04:38.051
Name each dependency explicitly, and keep
labels and representations consistent

56
00:04:38.051 --> 00:04:40.135
throughout the analysis.

57
00:04:40.250 --> 00:04:48.243
Stress-test the reasoning around ACM Code of
Ethics Foundations with an edge case and

58
00:04:48.243 --> 00:04:51.668
identify which assumption controls the
outcome.

59
00:04:51.668 --> 00:04:54.762
Computing professionals should avoid harm.

60
00:04:54.762 --> 00:05:02.081
Consider this worked case: Students analyze a
case study where a developer must choose

61
00:05:02.081 --> 00:05:07.309
between meeting a tight deadline and
implementing robust security measures.

62
00:05:07.309 --> 00:05:13.918
Change one assumption at a time and observe
which part of the reasoning no longer holds.

63
00:05:13.918 --> 00:05:20.684
The edge case reveals the boundary of the
claim without discarding the useful core idea.

64
00:05:20.786 --> 00:05:27.922
Use Ethical Decision-Making Framework to
diagnose a failure by separating symptoms,

65
00:05:27.922 --> 00:05:30.517
causes, and corrective action.

66
00:05:30.517 --> 00:05:38.684
Ethical computing decisions identify
stakeholders, foreseeable benefits and harms,

67
00:05:38.684 --> 00:05:42.314
uncertainty, alternatives, and obligations.

68
00:05:42.314 --> 00:05:50.104
Consider this worked case: Students map out a
stakeholder impact chart for a proposed

69
00:05:50.104 --> 00:05:56.782
algorithm that processes user data, noting
potential privacy risks and mitigation steps.

70
00:05:56.782 --> 00:06:04.567
Locate the first point where observed behavior
diverges from expected behavior, then test the

71
00:06:04.567 --> 00:06:06.235
smallest plausible cause.

72
00:06:06.235 --> 00:06:13.751
The diagnosis is strongest when another person
can reproduce the observation and the test.

73
00:06:13.874 --> 00:06:20.324
Connect Responsible Technical Communication to
the earlier ideas and explain how the pieces

74
00:06:20.324 --> 00:06:22.308
work as one system.

75
00:06:22.308 --> 00:06:28.308
Responsible technical communication
distinguishes claims from evidence.

76
00:06:28.308 --> 00:06:36.245
Consider this worked case: Students practice
rewriting a draft abstract to separate

77
00:06:36.245 --> 00:06:42.198
empirical findings from theoretical hypotheses
and add proper citations.

78
00:06:42.199 --> 00:06:51.895
Explain each connection explicitly and verify
that information retains the same meaning

79
00:06:51.895 --> 00:06:53.511
across representations.

80
00:06:53.511 --> 00:07:00.933
The synthesis matters only if every connection
remains supported by the original evidence.

81
00:07:00.933 --> 00:07:07.844
Name each dependency explicitly, and keep
labels and representations consistent

82
00:07:07.844 --> 00:07:09.917
throughout the analysis.

83
00:07:10.034 --> 00:07:18.058
Finish with Research Integrity and Methodology
as a practical checklist that can guide a new

84
00:07:18.058 --> 00:07:18.593
analysis.

85
00:07:18.593 --> 00:07:26.377
Authors should preserve research integrity and
avoid plagiarism, fabrication, and

86
00:07:26.377 --> 00:07:27.155
falsification.

87
00:07:27.155 --> 00:07:34.435
Consider this worked case: Students draft a
methodology section that explicitly states

88
00:07:34.435 --> 00:07:41.108
sample sizes, tools used, and known
constraints of their experimental setup.

89
00:07:41.109 --> 00:07:49.057
For each checklist item, record the evidence,
the remaining uncertainty, and the next

90
00:07:49.057 --> 00:07:50.280
verification step.

91
00:07:50.280 --> 00:07:56.886
The result is a repeatable procedure, not a
conclusion that depends on memory or

92
00:07:56.886 --> 00:07:57.358
intuition.
